  from jsonb_array_elements('[{"n":1,"t":"single_choice","p":"A hospital records each patient''s blood group. Which classification is correct?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A hospital records each patient''s blood group. Which classification is correct?"}]}]},"e":"Blood group is a label, not a number: it is qualitative (categorical) data. It cannot be ordered or averaged, which is why a bar chart is appropriate and a mean is not.","m":2,"df":"medium","tp":"classification of data","st":"qualitative versus quantitative","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp4-mock","question_number":1,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"A","strand":"","next_time_hint":"Ask whether the values are labels or amounts. If you cannot sensibly average them, they are qualitative.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp4-mock","option_labels":["Qualitative (categorical), and a bar chart is appropriate","Quantitative continuous, because blood is measured","Quantitative discrete, because there are four groups","Qualitative, and the mean blood group should be calculated"],"option_key":[1],"hints":[]},"marking":""},{"n":2,"t":"single_choice","p":"The salaries in a small company are $22 000, $24 000, $25 000, $26 000 and $180 000. Which statement best describes the effect of the $180 000 salary?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The salaries in a small company are "},{"type":"text","text":"$22 000, $"},{"type":"text","text":"24 000, "},{"type":"text","text":"$25 000, $"},{"type":"text","text":"26 000 and "},{"type":"text","text":"$180 000. Which statement best describes the effect of the $"},{"type":"text","text":"180 000 salary?"}]}]},"e":"The mean is 277 000 ÷ 5 = $55 400 while four of the five salaries are below $26 000, so the mean is pulled up by the outlier. The median ($25 000) is unaffected and describes a typical salary better.","m":3,"df":"medium","tp":"mean, mode and median","st":"outliers and choice of measure","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp4-mock","question_number":2,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"A","strand":"","next_time_hint":"An outlier moves the mean because the mean uses every value''s size; the median only uses position.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp4-mock","option_labels":["It inflates the mean, so the median describes a typical salary better","It inflates the median more than the mean","It has no effect because it is a single value","It makes the mode the best measure of centre"],"option_key":[1],"hints":[]},"marking":""},{"n":3,"t":"single_choice","p":"A grouped frequency table has classes 0 ≤ x < 10, 10 ≤ x < 20, 20 ≤ x < 30 with frequencies 5, 9 and 6. Which midpoint must be used for the class 10 ≤ x < 20 when estimating the mean?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A grouped frequency table has classes 0 ≤ x < 10, 10 ≤ x < 20, 20 ≤ x < 30 with frequencies 5, 9 and 6. Which midpoint must be used for the class 10 ≤ x < 20 when estimating the mean?"}]}]},"e":"The midpoint of a class is the average of its boundaries: (10 + 20)/2 = 15. Using 10 or 20 would place every value in that class at a boundary, which systematically skews the estimate.","m":2,"df":"hard","tp":"grouped data","st":"class midpoints","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp4-mock","question_number":3,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"A","strand":"","next_time_hint":"Midpoint = (lower boundary + upper boundary) ÷ 2, not one of the boundaries.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp4-mock","option_labels":["15","10, the lower boundary","20, the upper boundary","9, the frequency"],"option_key":[1],"hints":[]},"marking":""},{"n":4,"t":"single_choice","p":"A histogram has one class of width 5 with frequency 20 and an adjacent class of width 10 with frequency 20. The bar for the narrower class is drawn taller. By what factor?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A histogram has one class of width 5 with frequency 20 and an adjacent class of width 10 with frequency 20. The bar for the narrower class is drawn taller. By what factor?"}]}]},"e":"Frequency density is frequency ÷ width: 20/5 = 4 for the narrow class and 20/10 = 2 for the wide one, so the narrow bar is twice as tall. Equal frequencies do NOT give equal bar heights when widths differ.","m":3,"df":"hard","tp":"histograms","st":"frequency density with unequal widths","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp4-mock","question_number":4,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"A","strand":"","next_time_hint":"In a histogram, area — not height — represents frequency. Height = frequency ÷ class width.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp4-mock","option_labels":["2","1 — equal frequencies must give equal bars","0.5","4"],"option_key":[1],"hints":[]},"marking":""},{"n":5,"t":"single_choice","p":"A cumulative frequency curve shows 40 students in total. Reading across at cumulative frequency 10 gives the value 23. What have you found?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A cumulative frequency curve shows 40 students in total. Reading across at cumulative frequency 10 gives the value 23. What have you found?"}]}]},"e":"The lower quartile is the value below which a quarter of the data lies: 40 ÷ 4 = 10, so the x-value at cumulative frequency 10 is Q1 = 23. The median would be read at 20 and Q3 at 30.","m":3,"df":"hard","tp":"cumulative frequency","st":"reading quartiles from a curve","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp4-mock","question_number":5,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"A","strand":"","next_time_hint":"On a cumulative frequency curve, read n/4, n/2 and 3n/4 on the vertical axis to find Q1, the median and Q3.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp4-mock","option_labels":["The lower quartile, Q1 = 23","The median, 23","The upper quartile, Q3 = 23","The interquartile range, 23"],"option_key":[1],"hints":[]},"marking":""},{"n":6,"t":"single_choice","p":"Two classes sit the same test. Class A has mean 62 and standard deviation 4; Class B has mean 62 and standard deviation 11. Which statement is correct?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Two classes sit the same test. Class A has mean 62 and standard deviation 4; Class B has mean 62 and standard deviation 11. Which statement is correct?"}]}]},"e":"A larger standard deviation means the values are more spread out around the same mean. Both classes average 62, but Class B''s marks are far more variable, so individual performances are less predictable.","m":3,"df":"hard","tp":"standard deviation","st":"interpreting standard deviation","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp4-mock","question_number":6,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"A","strand":"","next_time_hint":"Standard deviation measures spread, not level. Equal means say nothing about consistency.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp4-mock","option_labels":["Class B has more variable marks, so its results are less consistent","Class B has a higher average mark","The standard deviations show Class A has more outliers","The two classes performed identically because the means are equal"],"option_key":[1],"hints":[]},"marking":""},{"n":7,"t":"single_choice","p":"A stem-and-leaf diagram has rows 3 | 2 4 6 and 4 | 1 1 1 5 with the key ''4 | 1 means 41''. What is the mode?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A stem-and-leaf diagram has rows 3 | 2 4 6 and 4 | 1 1 1 5 with the key ''4 | 1 means 41''. What is the mode?"}]}]},"e":"The leaf 1 appears three times on stem 4, giving the value 41 three times — more often than any other value. Reading the key correctly is essential: on stem 4 the leaf 1 is 41, not 1.","m":3,"df":"hard","tp":"stem-and-leaf diagrams","st":"reading a stem-and-leaf key","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp4-mock","question_number":7,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"A","strand":"","next_time_hint":"Always read the key first. The stem supplies the tens and the leaf the units in this example.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp4-mock","option_labels":["41","1","46 (the highest value on the first row)","There is no mode"],"option_key":[1],"hints":[]},"marking":""},{"n":8,"t":"single_choice","p":"A box plot has minimum 12, Q1 18, median 25, Q3 31 and maximum 60. Which statement best explains the shape of the distribution?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A box plot has minimum 12, Q1 18, median 25, Q3 31 and maximum 60. Which statement best explains the shape of the distribution?"}]}]},"e":"The upper whisker runs from 31 to 60, which is much longer than the lower whisker (12 to 18), and the median is closer to Q1. That indicates positive skew: a tail of high values.","m":3,"df":"hard","tp":"five-number summary and box plots","st":"shape from a box plot","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp4-mock","question_number":8,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"A","strand":"","next_time_hint":"Compare whisker lengths and the position of the median inside the box; that is the evidence for skew.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp4-mock","option_labels":["It is positively skewed — the upper whisker is much longer","It is symmetrical because the median lies inside the box","It is negatively skewed because the minimum is small","The shape cannot be judged from a box plot"],"option_key":[1],"hints":[]},"marking":""},{"n":9,"t":"single_choice","p":"Eight students scored 3, 5, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9, 9 on a quiz. A ninth student scores 10. How does the median change?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Eight students scored 3, 5, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9, 9 on a quiz. A ninth student scores 10. How does the median change?"}]}]},"e":"With eight values the median is (6 + 7)/2 = 6.5. Adding a ninth value gives 3, 5, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9, 9, 10, whose median is the 5th value = 7. The mean rises by less than a mark, but the median moves to an actual data value.","m":3,"df":"medium","tp":"mean, mode and median","st":"effect of n on the median","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp4-mock","question_number":9,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"A","strand":"","next_time_hint":"For an even number of values the median is the mean of the two middle values; with an odd number it is a single value.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp4-mock","option_labels":["It rises from 6.5 to 7","It stays at 6.5","It rises from 6.5 to 7.5","It falls because more values are added"],"option_key":[1],"hints":[]},"marking":""},{"n":10,"t":"single_choice","p":"A cumulative frequency table ends with a total frequency of 50. A candidate reads the median at cumulative frequency 50. What error have they made?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A cumulative frequency table ends with a total frequency of 50. A candidate reads the median at cumulative frequency 50. What error have they made?"}]}]},"e":"The median is read at n/2 = 25, not at the total. Cumulating to 50 locates the maximum, so the candidate has found the largest value rather than the middle one.","m":3,"df":"hard","tp":"cumulative frequency","st":"misreading a cumulative axis","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp4-mock","question_number":10,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"A","strand":"","next_time_hint":"The vertical axis of a cumulative frequency diagram runs to n, so quartiles are read at n/4, n/2 and 3n/4.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp4-mock","option_labels":["They read the maximum instead of the median; the median is at 25","They should have read at 50 ÷ 4","Nothing — the median is always at the total","They should have subtracted 50 from the total"],"option_key":[1],"hints":[]},"marking":""},{"n":11,"t":"single_choice","p":"For the grouped table 0 ≤ x < 10, 10 ≤ x < 20, 20 ≤ x < 30 with frequencies 4, 7, 5, what is the estimated mean?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"For the grouped table 0 ≤ x < 10, 10 ≤ x < 20, 20 ≤ x < 30 with frequencies 4, 7, 5, what is the estimated mean?"}]}]},"e":"Σf x = 4(5) + 7(15) + 5(25) = 20 + 105 + 125 = 250 and Σf = 16, so the estimated mean is 250 ÷ 16 = 15.625.","m":3,"df":"hard","tp":"grouped data","st":"estimated mean from midpoints","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp4-mock","question_number":11,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"B","strand":"","next_time_hint":"Estimated mean = Σ(f × midpoint) ÷ Σf. Write both sums before dividing.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp4-mock","option_labels":["15.625","15 (the middle class midpoint)","16 (Σf ÷ 1, a misread)","20 (the highest midpoint)"],"option_key":[1],"hints":[]},"marking":""},{"n":12,"t":"single_choice","p":"A histogram bar covers 20 ≤ x < 30 with height (frequency density) 1.4. What is the frequency of that class?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A histogram bar covers 20 ≤ x < 30 with height (frequency density) 1.4. What is the frequency of that class?"}]}]},"e":"Frequency = density × class width = 1.4 × 10 = 14. The area of the bar is the frequency, which is why density must be multiplied by the width.","m":3,"df":"hard","tp":"histograms","st":"frequency from density","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp4-mock","question_number":12,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"B","strand":"","next_time_hint":"frequency = frequency density × class width; equivalently, the area of each bar.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp4-mock","option_labels":["14","1.4","28","140"],"option_key":[1],"hints":[]},"marking":""},{"n":13,"t":"single_choice","p":"A data set is 4, 4, 4, 4, 4. What is its standard deviation, and what does that tell you?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A data set is 4, 4, 4, 4, 4. What is its standard deviation, and what does that tell you?"}]}]},"e":"Every value equals the mean 4, so each deviation is 0, Σ(x − x̄)² = 0 and σ = 0. A standard deviation of zero means there is no spread at all.","m":2,"df":"hard","tp":"standard deviation","st":"standard deviation of a constant set","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp4-mock","question_number":13,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"B","strand":"","next_time_hint":"Standard deviation is the root mean square deviation; if there are no deviations, it is zero.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp4-mock","option_labels":["0 — there is no spread","4 — the mean is 4","2 — half the mean","It cannot be calculated from five values"],"option_key":[1],"hints":[]},"marking":""},{"n":14,"t":"single_choice","p":"Two runners'' times (minutes) over 6 races: A: 30, 32, 33, 34, 36, 37. B: 28, 31, 33, 33, 35, 44. Which comparison is supported by the data?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Two runners'' times (minutes) over 6 races: A: 30, 32, 33, 34, 36, 37. B: 28, 31, 33, 33, 35, 44. Which comparison is supported by the data?"}]}]},"e":"Both have the same mean (202 ÷ 6 ≈ 33.67) and the same median (33), but B has a much larger spread (range 16 against 7, and larger deviations), so B is less consistent even though the averages match.","m":3,"df":"hard","tp":"comparing data sets","st":"comparing centre and spread","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp4-mock","question_number":14,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"B","strand":"","next_time_hint":"Compare a measure of centre AND a measure of spread; two distributions can share a centre and differ in consistency.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp4-mock","option_labels":["The medians are equal but B is less consistent","B has the higher median","A has the larger range","A is less consistent because its values are closer together"],"option_key":[1],"hints":[]},"marking":""},{"n":15,"t":"single_choice","p":"A line graph of a shop''s monthly sales rises steeply from January to March, then is flat from March to August. Which conclusion is justified?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A line graph of a shop''s monthly sales rises steeply from January to March, then is flat from March to August. Which conclusion is justified?"}]}]},"e":"The graph supports a statement about the trend in the recorded months only: sales grew early in the year and then held steady. It does not explain why, and it says nothing about September onwards.","m":3,"df":"hard","tp":"line graphs","st":"trend versus cause","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp4-mock","question_number":15,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"B","strand":"","next_time_hint":"A graph shows what happened in the data, not why. Keep causal claims out of a data interpretation.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp4-mock","option_labels":["Sales increased early in the year and then stayed roughly constant","The shop will close because sales stopped growing","The shop''s advertising caused the March increase","Sales were highest in August"],"option_key":[1],"hints":[]},"marking":""},{"n":16,"t":"single_choice","p":"A market researcher records four variables. Which one of them is continuous data?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A market researcher records four variables. Which one of them is continuous data?"}]}]},"e":"The mass of a parcel can take any value in an interval, so it is continuous. Shoe size, the number of siblings and a house number are counts or labels.","m":3,"df":"hard","tp":"classification of data","st":"discrete versus continuous","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp4-mock","question_number":16,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"B","strand":"","next_time_hint":"If the value can, in principle, be measured more and more precisely, it is continuous; if you count it, it is discrete.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp4-mock","option_labels":["The mass of a parcel, measured to the nearest gram","A shoe size","The number of siblings a student has","A house number"],"option_key":[1],"hints":[]},"marking":""},{"n":17,"t":"single_choice","p":"For the data 3, 5, 7, 8, 10, 12, 14, 15, 18 (using the median-excluded quartile method), what is the interquartile range?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"For the data 3, 5, 7, 8, 10, 12, 14, 15, 18 (using the median-excluded quartile method), what is the interquartile range?"}]}]},"e":"There are 9 values, so the median is the 5th value = 10. The lower half is 3, 5, 7, 8 with Q1 = 6, and the upper half is 12, 14, 15, 18 with Q3 = 14.5. IQR = 14.5 − 6 = 8.5.","m":3,"df":"hard","tp":"five-number summary and box plots","st":"quartiles with an odd n","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp4-mock","question_number":17,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"B","strand":"","next_time_hint":"Split the ordered data at the median and exclude the median itself when n is odd, then average the middle two of each half.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp4-mock","option_labels":["8.5","10","15 (maximum − minimum)","5 (Q3 − median)"],"option_key":[1],"hints":[]},"marking":""},{"n":18,"t":"single_choice","p":"A grouped table has classes with widths 10, 10, 20 and 20 and frequencies 6, 9, 10 and 6. Which statement about drawing a histogram is correct?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A grouped table has classes with widths 10, 10, 20 and 20 and frequencies 6, 9, 10 and 6. Which statement about drawing a histogram is correct?"}]}]},"e":"Because the widths differ, the bar heights must be frequency densities: 0.6, 0.9, 0.5 and 0.3. Using the raw frequencies would make the 20-wide classes look twice as important as they are.","m":3,"df":"hard","tp":"grouped data","st":"density for mixed widths","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp4-mock","question_number":18,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"B","strand":"","next_time_hint":"Divide each frequency by its own class width before drawing, so that all areas remain comparable.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp4-mock","option_labels":["The four densities are 0.6, 0.9, 0.5 and 0.3","The bars should be 6, 9, 10 and 6 high","All four bars are the same height because the total is 31","The widths cannot be compared on a histogram"],"option_key":[1],"hints":[]},"marking":""}]'::jsonb) as x
